    <!-- Pace message (random pick within each mood) -->
    <string name="pace_empty_1">Zeus sees everything. Except your breakfast — that one you log yourself.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_2">Herodotus wrote down everything he saw. Be Herodotus.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_3">Odysseus wandered ten years and came home with a full story. Your morning so far: blank.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_4">Pythagoras saw numbers in everything. Your diary would break his heart.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_5">Plato\'s cave had more going on than this diary. Log something and step into the light.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_6">Your diary is as blank as an unwritten epic. Sing, muse, of breakfast.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_7">Even the Delphic Oracle wants an offering first. Feed her one entry.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_1">Right on plan. Somewhere on Olympus, a god just nodded.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_2">Keep this pace and Pindar writes you an ode.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_3">The Fates checked your thread today: not a single knot. Carry on.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_4">Zeno proved motion is impossible. Your steady pace just proved him wrong.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_5">Balanced like a Greek philosopher. Nothing in excess.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_6">Delphi carved it in stone: nothing in excess. You\'re doing it in an app.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_7">This is Aristotle\'s golden mean. Try not to let it go to your head.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_1">Easy, Achilles. Save some rage — and some calories — for the evening.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_2">Eating like Heracles mid-labor. The labor, so far, is the couch.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_3">This hour\'s budget is gone. Even Croesus ran out eventually.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_4">Ahead of the plan again? Icarus was ahead of the plan too.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_5">That snack was pure hubris. You know who comes next.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_6">Charging like a hoplite at Marathon. There are 40 more kilometres — slow down.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_7">The Sirens are singing about snacks. Tie yourself to the mast.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_1">The goal fell like a tragic hero. Curtain. Kitchen closed.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_2">The Trojans also said one more won\'t hurt. About the horse.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_3">Hippocrates looked at your day and prescribed: water, silence, tomorrow.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_4">Impressive. Even Dionysus would call it a night at this point.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_5">Tomorrow the scale judges you like Minos. Sleep well.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_6">You sailed past Scylla straight into Charybdis. Charybdis had dessert.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_7">Tantalus at least couldn\'t reach the food. You had no such excuse.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_gentle_1">Nothing logged yet — no hurry. Even the Muses wait for the first line.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_gentle_2">Even the Parthenon began with a single stone. One entry is enough.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_gentle_3">The diary is quiet so far. Philosophers valued a slow morning — just don\'t forget lunch.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_gentle_4">Even Socrates began by admitting he knew nothing. Start with one entry.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_gentle_5">Diaita is a way of living, not a single meal. Begin whenever you like.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_gentle_1">Lovely pacing. The Greeks had a word for this: harmonia.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_gentle_2">Wise, measured, on plan. Very Athena of you.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_gentle_3">A day like a calm Aegean — smooth sailing so far.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_gentle_4">Nothing in excess, as they carved at Delphi. You are living it today.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_gentle_5">Steady as a temple column. Quietly well done.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_gentle_1">A little ahead of plan for this hour. Even the gods paced their feasts.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_gentle_2">This hour took a bit more than its share. Hippocrates would suggest a lighter next meal.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_gentle_3">Slightly ahead of schedule. Odysseus also took the long way home — just don\'t stop at every island.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_gentle_4">Aristotle\'s golden mean is just behind you — drift back toward it.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_gentle_5">Marathon was won by pacing, not sprinting. Ease off a little.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_gentle_1">Over the goal today — it happens. Heraclitus promised a new river tomorrow.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_gentle_2">Above the goal — it happens. Even Epicurus allowed the occasional feast.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_gentle_3">Over the target today. Even Homer nods — the epic continues tomorrow.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_gentle_4">Icarus flew high once and learned. Tomorrow, fly a little lower.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_gentle_5">Even Olympians had feast days. The plan waits for you tomorrow.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_1">Sulking in your tent like Achilles? Even he came back. Log your food.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_2">Know thyself, said Delphi. Start with what you ate. Now.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_3">Xenophon kept records for ten thousand men in retreat. You can log one sandwich.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_4">Even Charon logs every passenger. One coin, one line. Your turn.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_5">The Greeks wrote epics about deeds. Opening the app and leaving is not a deed.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_6">Spartans didn\'t debate breakfast, they ate and marched. Log it.</string>
    <string name="pace_empty_brutal_7">An empty tablet impresses no one, not even Diogenes. Write something.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_1">On plan. Good. Olympus is watching — don\'t embarrass us both.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_2">Acceptable. Barely. The agoge would still make you run five more laps.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_3">On plan. Suspicious. Last time things looked this good, there was a horse involved.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_4">Discipline detected. Suspicious, but the ephors approve. For now.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_5">You\'re on plan. The fridge stays shut — consider that a prophecy.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_6">Discipline worthy of a phalanx. Hold the line until midnight.</string>
    <string name="pace_ok_brutal_7">Leonidas would grunt approvingly. Barely. Don\'t ruin it.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_1">This hour\'s budget: annihilated. Kronos ate his children with more restraint.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_2">You\'re eating like the day has 40 hours. Not even Zeus can grant you that. Stop.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_3">Ahead of schedule again. Your dinner is now a myth. Myths don\'t fill stomachs.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_4">That was not a snack, that was the sacking of a city. Lock it down.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_5">Pheidippides also went out too fast. He announced the victory and died. Pace yourself.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_6">This is not a feast at Olympia. Ration yourself, athlete.</string>
    <string name="pace_warn_brutal_7">Sparta had no word for second helping. Learn from them.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_1">Goal destroyed. The kitchen is now an archaeological site. Closed to visitors.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_2">Twelve labors? You did them all at the dinner table. Kitchen\'s closed, Heracles.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_3">\"Tonight we dine in Hades\" was a battle cry, not a meal plan. Water. Bed.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_4">The goal is dead by your hand. Sophocles would call it a tragedy. Go walk it off.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_5">Medusa turned men to stone with a look. Wait until the scale sees you tomorrow. Mouth closed.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_6">You ate like a Persian king and marched like nobody. Sit down.</string>
    <string name="pace_over_brutal_7">Sisyphus starts the hill again tomorrow. So do you.</string>

